 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **MobileNeRF**: Training uses larger MLPs (384 hidden units) but excludes them from inference to save time [S1]. Texture storage is constrained to powers of 2 up to $4096 \times 4096$ [S1].
-*   **Pyramid NeRF**: Enhances training speed by 15x and inference speed by 805x relative to original NeRF [S2].
-*   **Residual-NeRF-S**: Simpler merge strategy than Mixnet, but produces worse depth and view reconstruction, especially for partially opaque objects [S3].
-*   **OccNet**: Single-level version without positional encodings achieves 13.90 PSNR / 0.437 SSIM on 2D images and 20.60 PSNR / 0.849 SSIM on radiance fields, with training times of 02:17 and 100:23, and model sizes of 0.38 and 0.43 [S4, unofficial].
+* **MobileNeRF**: Training uses larger MLPs (384 hidden units) but excludes them from inference to save time [S1]. Texture storage is constrained to powers of 2 up to $4096 \times 4096$ [S1]. Strengths: Hardware-friendly rasterization pipeline. Limitations: Texture size constraints. When to use: Mobile or constrained hardware deployments [S1].
+* **Pyramid NeRF**: Enhances training speed by 15x and inference speed by 805x relative to original NeRF [S2]. Strengths: Rapid convergence and high inference throughput. Limitations: Progressive segmentation may introduce artifacts at frequency boundaries. When to use: Large-scale scenes requiring fast iteration [S2].
+* **Residual-NeRF-S**: Simpler merge strategy than Mixnet, but produces worse depth and view reconstruction, especially for partially opaque objects [S3]. Strengths: Reduced computational overhead. Limitations: Degraded transparency handling. When to use: Static background scenes without complex transparent objects [S3].
+* **OccNet**: Single-level version without positional encodings achieves 13.90 PSNR / 0.437 SSIM on 2D images and 20.60 PSNR / 0.849 SSIM on radiance fields, with training times of 02:17 and 100:23, and model sizes of 0.38 and 0.43 [S4, unofficial]. Strengths: Minimal parameter count. Limitations: Poor high-frequency reconstruction. When to use: Baseline comparisons or low-detail scenes [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF architectures evolved from the standard two-MLP design [Parent](11313-model-architecture-modifications.md). MobileNeRF adapts the network for texture-based feature storage with increased hidden layers for training-only MLPs [S1]. Pyramid NeRF introduces frequency optimization and progressive MLP segmentation for rapid training and inference [S2]. Residual-NeRF-S simplifies the background-residual merge via direct addition and sigmoid activation [S3]. OccNet serves as a single-level baseline without positional encodings [S4, unofficial].
